{"entityMap":{},"blocks":[{"key":"fg3e6","text":"Random Float Generation","type":"header-one","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3og90","text":"In a previous article we hand rolled our own RNG, to improve on what the C standard library `rand()` gives us.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1384p","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"atrjv","text":"We also talked about how the floating point number works.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"19cfl","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fci93","text":"In this article, I want to combine the two. I want to find out how we can generate random floating point numbers between 0 and 1, and improve our rng library.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6qonh","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"d44i5","text":"The naive way","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dvd50","text":"We already created an `rng_f()` function when we set up the rng.c lib. But it generates random floats by dividing a random int by the max:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6k474","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"cdano","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4bc7t","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3nitn","text":"This approach has a small bug. We want to generate in the range of [0.0, 1.0) so we want to be able to reach 1.0, but never hit it.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9v83","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3fi9j","text":"Because of rounding, there is actually a chance where we do hit 1.0.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dl4rn","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"bfdso","text":"About 1 in ... million. This seems insignificant, but we were already generating millions of random numbers. The chance of it happening is quite high in that situation.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"e8pln","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"14gn5","text":"Looking at float bits","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4eqjo","text":"Let's reflect back on how floats work in memory.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"ek61i","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"c5th9","text":"The mantissa (23 bits) can get you from a base number all the way (but not including) double that base number.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"c9euv","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"cu4hn","text":"This means that the smallest \"step\" we can take from a certain float, depends on the size or exponent of the float. Floats close to zero have very tiny granular steps. Large floats have a larger step size:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[{"offset":93,"length":8,"style":"ITALIC"}],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"27lul","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"24kei","text":"[drawing where you see the increasing resolution]","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"18s8e","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"f3dv","text":"For example, the number 1.0 is a certain exponent (01111111) with the mantissa set to all zeros:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"b3iaq","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"474jf","text":"[bit representation of 1.0]","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6ev1k","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3bbk8","text":"The closest float before hitting 2.0 has the same exponent as 1.0, but with the mantissa fully maxed out (all 1s):","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8768d","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8bd1","text":"[bit representation of 1.9999999]","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"bpt8l","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"a83u3","text":"So when we randomize the mantissa bits, we can get a random float. As long as we keep the exponent bits constant, we know we have a consistent step size.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"cpp76","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8gbhr","text":"Randomising the 23 mantissa bits","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"133jl","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9i436","text":"In the [floating point hacking article], we explored what the bits of a float mean, and how we can flip them.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"d3tt5","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"55aro","text":"If we have an RNG that gives us some random bits, we can use them to randomise our float bits.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1jokl","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fobu5","text":"So if we want to randomize the 23 mantissa bits of the float, this is how we can do that:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"ff17u","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"49aqo","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"e1qoa","text":"typedef union {","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"cdhvg","text":"  float f;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dbkq","text":"  uint32 u;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"bnuo8","text":"} Bits;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"beola","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1ddn0","text":"Bits bits = {0};","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"p2cn","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dr8at","text":"bits.u |= rng_u() >> (32 - 23);","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9pi1v","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"d2tfu","text":"Here, we use the union trick to be able to access float memory as if it's an integer. This allows for bitwise operations. Then we take a random 32 bit integer from `rng_u()`, shift it down 9 places to the right (32 - 23). We do this shift such that we zero out the first 9 bits, so we don't flip any of the first 9 bits of the float.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"btf7h","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"99tg7","text":"If you start at the number 0, and you randomize the mantissa bits, you get a random range between 0.0 and ","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"f15kl","text":"~0.0000000000000000000000000000000000000118.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"ecmf7","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dtchn","text":"Not very practical for our target range of [0, 1) I reckon. This is because of that variable resolution that we get in floats.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"ee3np","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fluof","text":"If we start with the number 0.5 though, and randomize the mantissa, we achieve a range of [0.5, 1.0). That's starting to look more like it.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2kise","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6tq6t","text":"But starting at 0.5 gives us a total \"range length\" of 0.5 (1 - 0.5 = 0.5). The target range of 0 to 1 has a length of 1. So what if we start from 1?","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2p8vu","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"kqr6","text":"Then the range becomes [1, 2). So we get the desired length of the range, it's only shifted up by 1. If we then subtract 1, we land exactly in range [0, 1). This is what that looks like in C:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"c56u5","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6cfkh","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7j97s","text":"Bits bits = {.f = 1.0f};","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9avoo","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4a9mn","text":"bits.u |= rng_u() >> (32 - 23);","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"i3hs","text":"bits.f -= 1.0f;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7lqp5","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7no9a","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"21h9u","text":"We start from a base of 1.0f. Then randomize the lowest 23 bits (mantissa bits) to vary our range between 1 and 2 (not including 2). Finally we subtract one, which is an acceptable operation to do.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9n8j5","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dh846","text":"Now we have a very efficient way to turn a random 32 bit int into a random 32 bit float between 0 and 1.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5j356","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"f6vee","text":"But, we can do better.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7sd2h","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4p811","text":"Doubling the resolution","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6kd3b","text":"Let's take a closer look at the structure of a float.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7taj5","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5l2nb","text":"We want equally spaced out floats in the range of 0 to 1. This is not trivial, because if you consider ALL floats between 0 and 1, you get a large variety of step sizes:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"eng04","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4j7hh","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"afbct","text":"The steps close to 0.0 are a lot more fine-grained than the steps close to 1. So which step size do we choose? We choose the smallest one that works everywhere between 0 - 1. That means we should choose the step size of the range [0.5, 1).","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"71r86","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"51ie1","text":"Because if you go down the exponent, closer to zero, you keep dividing by 2. So the step size between [0.25, 0.5) is half the step size of [0.5, 1). This is  fine, we just have to pick every other step between [0.25, 0.5) to stick to a constant step size. Same for the range below that, [0.125, 0.25), we pick every 4th step, and so on.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8p6jf","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dnc52","text":"So what does this mean? The step size we can take is the smalles increment between the [0.5, 1) range. We can calculate that by manipulating bits:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"bme2l","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"efj9j","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5dcgc","text":"Bits bits = {.f = 0.5f};","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"b495q","text":"bits.u += 1;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7fsek","text":"float step_size = bits.f - 0.5f;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6ji2t","text":"printf(\"step size: %f (%a)\", step_size, step_size);","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5oi0o","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"bij1m","text":"This gives us an ideal step size of around  `0.0000000596`, or more exact `0x1p-24`, so `1 * 2 ^ -24`.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"403d3","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2uvn7","text":"What is the step size we're currently using when we generate floats using the random mantissa and subtract 1 method?","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"f2pg0","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4icim","text":"That step size is twice as big, because we're generating a float between [1, 2), which is the next level up from [0.5, 1).","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6sk7","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2eev8","text":"So although that method works, we're throwing away half of our possible floating point numbers between 0, 1 that we could have had..","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7vblg","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"e9bje","text":"Instead of 23 bits of randomness, we could add a 24th bit and double our resolution.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9jk72","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7osaq","text":"How can we do this? We could go back to starting from 0.5, randomising the mantissa to get the range [0.5, 1). And then with 50% chance, subtract 0.5f from that. That could definitely work, but there is a way more simple and boring solution that we'll explore next.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"avqkf","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"f2d6a","text":"Back to basics","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2l6hk","text":"We looked at the ideal step size for the range of random numbers to pick from between 0 and 1. This turned out to be the number 2^-24 or 1 / 2^24.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fnkhe","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"b9emu","text":"We've also seen how we can generate 32 random bits, and taking 23 of them to randomize bits in a float.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"83agp","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6p440","text":"What if we took an extra random bit, 24 in total? This gives us an integer range of [0, 2^24). The 2^24 is not included, because if you have 24 bits maxed out (all 1s), it equals 2^24 - 1.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"e0gq6","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dg8r2","text":"We can recognise that 2^24 / 2^24 = 1, obviously. And since we don't quite reach 2^24, we get (2^24 - 1) / 2^24 which is almost 1 (one step below 1). You might also realise that if we divide the integer value of our 24 random bits by 2^24, we end up in our desired range of [0, 1).","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1ij1i","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"daos8","text":"Dividing by 2^24 is the same as multiplying by 2^-24 (our step size). So let's do that, as multiplication is more efficient than division. So now it all comes together. Since the step size of our integer range is 1, if we multiply by 2^-24, this is the new step size.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"3deod","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5j8rj","text":"So the improved method for converting a 32 bit integer to a 32 bit float in range [0, 1) becomes:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"effg8","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5jq76","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7stm1","text":"// Generate 24 random bits","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7pj0r","text":"uint32_t bits = rng_u() >> (32 - 24);","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2gnrk","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6laoq","text":"// Multiply by the smallest step size to generate a float between 0-1","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2q4eu","text":"float f = bits * 0x1.0p-24f;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"a6l38","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2om20","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"68hvp","text":"That's all. This magical looking `0x1.0p-24f` is hexadecimal float notation. It allows us to specify an exact float value.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"a9gc7","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dr42n","text":"The start `0x` means we're about to write a hexadecimal number. The `1.0` is the mantissa in hex format (but in decimal format 1.0 is the same). And finally `p-24` is the \"power\" or exponent (p instead of e, because e is part of hexadecimal characters). It ends with an `f` to make sure it's a 32 bit float and not a 64 bit double.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4pm0r","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6or4k","text":"Tucking it into the RNG library","type":"header-two","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"79iac","text":"With this method of converting  32 random bits to a clean float in [0, 1), let's integrate it into our library.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7ujo","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4cpp6","text":"This is what our random integer function looks like:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"9u8v7","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"5mhe9","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"2d286","text":"static inline uint32_t rng_u(void) {","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"c5r87","text":"  return pcg32_random_r(&pcg32_global);","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"e2pd6","text":"}","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"chnf","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"7fop0","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1g8sv","text":"Let's create an `rng_f()` function in the same vein:","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"fs3td","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"cfb46","text":"```c","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"40md","text":"static inline float rng_f(void) {","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1khdu","text":"  return (pcg32_random_r(&pcg32_global) >> 8) * 0x1.0p-24f;","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6e9pt","text":"}","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"8sasj","text":"```","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"1cf9h","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"27iug","text":"Instead of calling `rng_u()` we call `pcg32_random_r` to reduce indirection.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"6ernl","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"frcr6","text":"And we shift 8 bits, that's like saying \"discard the 8 lowest bits\" so we're left with 24 bits.","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"4m8f9","text":"","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}},{"key":"dd4le","text":"And that's it! You can check out the code in the [rng.c repo]. If it looks a bit different by the time you read this, that might be because I've improved some things :)","type":"unstyled","depth":0,"inlineStyleRanges":[],"entityRanges":[],"data":{}}]}